[?25l[0m[38;5;141m> [0m[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong and what I changed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mThe bug:[22m An off-by-one element indexing error in the [38;5;10mhmput[0m and [38;5;10mhmget[0m wrapper functions.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mRoot cause:[22m The stb_ds library uses a layout where the hash map pointer [38;5;10mt[0m points to element[1] of the underlying array (element[0] is a "default" entry). The [38;5;10mstbds_hmput_key[0m function stores the index of a newly inserted entry in the [38;5;10mtemp[0m field as [38;5;10mi - 1[0m, where [38;5;10mi[0m is the index relative to the array base. The C macros then access the entry as [38;5;10m(t)[stbds_temp((t)-1)][0m — indexing from [38;5;10mt[0m (the hash pointer), not from the raw array base. Since [38;5;10mt = arr + 1_element[0m, [38;5;10mt[i-1][0m correctly resolves to [38;5;10marr[i][0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust translation was indexing from [38;5;10marr[0m (the raw array base) instead of from [38;5;10m*intmap[0m (the hash pointer [38;5;10mt[0m):[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mrust
[0m[38;5;10m// WRONG: indexes from arr, giving arr[i-1] (one element too early)
let entry = arr.add(INTMAP_ELEMSIZE * idx as usize) as *mut IntMapEntry;

// CORRECT: indexes from *intmap (= t), giving t[i-1] = arr[i]
let entry = (*intmap).add(INTMAP_ELEMSIZE * idx as usize) as *mut IntMapEntry;
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This caused [38;5;10mhmput[0m to write key/value pairs to the wrong memory location, and [38;5;10mhmget[0m to read from the wrong location, corrupting the hash map and returning incorrect values.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mThe fix:[22m Changed both [38;5;10mhmput[0m and [38;5;10mhmget[0m to index from [38;5;10m*intmap[0m instead of [38;5;10marr[0m — a two-line change.[0m[0m
